<p>“The tribal election in Macy happens the same day, and sometimes people vote in the tribal election and then forget to vote in the general election, and vice versa. And people not interested in voting, unfortunately,” said Steven Schneider, the Thurston County election commissioner.</p>

<p>Low voter turnout is not a new issue in Thurston County.</p>

<p>From 2021 to 2023, the Winnebago and Omaha tribes of Nebraska <a href="https://www.narf.org/nill/documents/20230119winnebago-thurston-nebraska-complaint.pdf">sued</a> Thurston County three times for Section 2 violations of the Voters Rights Act. These lawsuits all dealt with <a href="https://narf.org/cases/thurston-nebraska-redistricting/">redistricting</a> plans that would have diluted the Native American vote. The lawsuits were successful and led to re-negotiated districts.</p>

<p>In 2024, State Sen. Jen Day of Omaha introduced <a href="https://nebraskalegislature.gov/FloorDocs/108/PDF/Intro/LB1262.pdf">LB 1262</a>. The purpose of the bill was to create a 14-person commission of tribal members, four from Thurston County, who would study election patterns and access on the reservations. However, this bill failed to advance in the state legislature.</p>

<p>In addition, neither Thurston nor Dakota County votes entirely by mail. During the 2024 presidential election, counties that did fully vote by mail had higher voter turnouts.</p>

