<p>While voter turnout in Dakota County is still lower than most of the state, Cherie Conley, the Dakota County election commissioner, said that participation this year increased from previous years. Voter turnout increased from 34% in the 2022 midterm to 49% in this midterm.<br><br>She said turnout may have increased because of contested races for South Sioux City Mayor, city council and a school bond issue.</p>

<p>Dakota County shifted 21% toward the Democratic party in the governor’s race and 23% towards the Democratic party in the attorney general’s race from its 2024 margin, according to data from the <a href="https://apnews.com/projects/elections-2026/nebraska-governor-results/">Associated Press</a>.</p>

<p>Across the state, counties with more registered Republicans saw lower voter turnouts. For example, in Arthur County, where 89% of voters are registered as Republicans, voter turnout decreased by about 6%.</p>

<p>In Thurston County only 38% of voters are registered as Republicans. In Dakota County, only 35% of voters are registered as Republicans. These two counties saw voter turnouts increase by about 10% for Thurston County and 15% for Dakota County.</p>

